Keith Steiger:
> The more the conversation wanders along these lines, the more I realize that
> we're running into the philosophical questions of how the monsters and items
> came to be where they are, and why they're so pristine until the moment you
> arrive, at which point they get tunneled and scorched and otherwise trashed.

Yup.  These are questions that can't be answered very sanely in the
context of nethack, other than to say that the player is the focus of
the game, the game world doesn't have a history or an ecology, and
these are its rules.  You can make incremental improvements in the
game's rationale, as the devteam has been doing, but I don't know if
it's possible to incrementally achieve a wholly reasonable universe.

Perhaps it's worth thinking about.

If you take the stance that nethack's universe does have a past, then
you need more types of stabilizing behavior: more organizing forces to
counteract the entropic forces.

A simple example: shopkeepers when they're idle could spread out piles
of items in their shop to fill empty spaces.  This would explain why
shop floors are always covered, but it wouldn't explain why there's
only one item per square.  This is a little more complicated, but it
can be tackled a couple different ways.

The problem is there are hundreds of instabilities in nethack.  Fixing
them all would be a substantial effort.

Maybe it would be easier to start over and design a new game with a
sound, stable-system basis.  It would take some time to evolve a new
game to the depth of nethack though..
